-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Replacement Policy
+
+The worker replaces cached sheets with a 2Q policy so that a long sequential screening — which touches many sheets once — cannot evict the smaller working set an editor is comparing. First-use sheets and reused sheets are held separately; only a sheet proven to be reused competes for the long-lived space.
+
+The policy keeps three structures, all private to the worker:
+
+- **Recent** — a FIFO queue of resident sheets admitted on their first render. Holds JPEG bytes.
+- **Reused** — an LRU queue of resident sheets that were requested again after aging out of Recent. Holds JPEG bytes.
+- **Ghost** — a FIFO list of cache *keys only* (clip ID, revision, renderer version) for sheets recently evicted out of Recent. Holds no JPEG bytes and so does not count against the 12 GiB.
+
+On a request for key `K`:
+
+- If `K` is resident in Reused, serve it and move it to the most-recently-used end of Reused.
+- If `K` is resident in Recent, serve it and leave its position in Recent unchanged. A repeat hit while still in Recent does not promote it — a sheet earns Reused by surviving eviction and being asked for again, not by being hit twice during one scan.
+- If `K` is not resident but is present in Ghost, the request is a proven reuse: render (or share the pending job), remove `K` from Ghost, and admit the completed sheet to the most-recently-used end of Reused.
+- If `K` is unknown (neither resident nor in Ghost), render (or share the pending job) and admit the completed sheet to the newest end of Recent.
+
+Reclamation runs before admitting any completed sheet, evicting until the admission fits within 12 GiB. Recent is held to a target share `Kin` of the byte budget; Reused holds the remainder. When Recent is at or above its share, the oldest sheet in Recent is evicted and its key is appended to Ghost (its bytes are dropped); otherwise the least-recently-used sheet in Reused is evicted outright. Ghost is bounded by key count `Kout`; its oldest keys are discarded past that bound, which only forfeits future reuse credit and frees no JPEG bytes.
+
+The default split is `Kin` = 3 GiB (one quarter of the budget) with `Kout` sized to the number of keys a full 12 GiB of typical 2 MiB sheets would occupy (~6,000). Both are worker-internal constants; changing them does not touch the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q (Recent/Reused/Ghost) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Protects the comparison working set from long sequential screenings by admitting a sheet to the long-lived queue only once it is reused after eviction. LRU is cheaper but a single screening scan can evict the comparison set; aged frequency counts retain popular sheets but add per-access counter maintenance and lag a project switch. The extra queues and the `Kin`/`Kout` split are accepted as the cost of scan resistance. |
